```java
class OrderService {
    void checkout(String type, double amount) {
        Payment payment;
        if (type.equals("card"))   payment = new CreditCardPayment(); // coupled
        else if (type.equals("paypal")) payment = new PayPalPayment();
        else throw new IllegalArgumentException(type);
        // This if-else is duplicated everywhere a Payment is needed.
        payment.processPayment(amount);
    }
}
```

```java
interface Payment { void processPayment(double amount); }
class CreditCardPayment implements Payment { public void processPayment(double a){ System.out.println("Card " + a); } }
class PayPalPayment    implements Payment { public void processPayment(double a){ System.out.println("PayPal " + a); } }

class PaymentFactory {
    static Payment create(String type) {
        return switch (type.toLowerCase()) {        // Java 17 switch expression
            case "card"   -> new CreditCardPayment();
            case "paypal" -> new PayPalPayment();
            default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown: " + type);
        };
    }
}

// Client is decoupled from concrete classes:
Payment payment = PaymentFactory.create("paypal");
payment.processPayment(99.99);
// Add UpiPayment -> one new class + one case. OrderService never changes.
```

*The creation decision now lives in one place instead of scattered `if-else` blocks.*

